    content: `Navigation deals with three slightly different "norths." TRUE (geographic) NORTH is the direction from where you stand to the geographic North Pole — the point the Earth rotates about. Lines of longitude (meridians) all run true north–south, and most map detail is oriented to it. MAGNETIC NORTH is the direction a compass needle actually points: toward the north magnetic pole, a moving spot in the Arctic Ocean produced by the Earth's molten-iron core dynamo, which drifts (currently toward Siberia at roughly 40–50 km/year) and whose pull direction varies from place to place. The horizontal angle between true north and magnetic north at your location is the MAGNETIC DECLINATION (called "variation" at sea). GRID NORTH is the direction the north–south grid lines on a topographic map point. Because a flat map uses a projection (e.g. Transverse Mercator), its straight grid lines cannot all follow the curved converging meridians, so grid north sits a fraction of a degree off true north (the "grid convergence"), and the angle between grid north and magnetic north is the "grid magnetic angle." A map's margin shows a diagram of all three and the current values. For most walking, grid and true north are close enough to treat as one; declination is the correction that genuinely matters.`,
