    content: `Magnetic declination (the same thing sailors call "variation") is the local angle between the north your MAP is drawn to (true or grid) and the north your COMPASS points to (magnetic). It is stated as so many degrees East or West, it varies with location — zero along the "agonic line," a few degrees over most of Western Europe, up to 20°+ in parts of North America and near the poles — and it drifts by fractions of a degree per year, so a map's printed value is only valid for a range of years (check a current source like NOAA/BGS). WHY IT MATTERS: when you measure a bearing on the map you get a grid bearing; when you set that number on your compass and walk, the compass aligns to magnetic north, so you actually walk off at the declination angle. A 2° error is about 35 m off per km; a 10° uncorrected declination is 175 m per km — enough to miss a hut or walk off a cliff in poor visibility. Correcting: going FROM map TO compass and FROM compass TO map you add in one direction and subtract in the other, depending on East/West declination. Common mnemonics: "Grid to Mag, ADD" / "Mag to Grid, get RID" (for west declination, UK); "East is least (subtract), West is best (add)" when converting true→magnetic in the US. Best of all, most baseplate compasses have an adjustable declination scale — set it once for your area and every bearing is automatically corrected.`,
