    content: `A back bearing (or reverse bearing) is simply the direction that points exactly opposite a given bearing — 180° around the compass. (It is a navigation term and has nothing to do with childbirth positions or selkie folklore.) Calculating it: if the forward bearing is less than 180°, add 180°; if it is 180° or more, subtract 180°. Examples: forward 040° → back 220°; forward 135° → back 315°; forward 300° → back 120°. Uses: (1) STAYING ON LINE — after walking away from a known point on a set bearing, turn around and sight back to that point; the needle should now read your back bearing, confirming you haven't drifted off. (2) RESECTION / TRIANGULATION — to fix your position, you take a bearing to a feature you can see and identify on the map, convert it to a grid bearing, then draw its BACK bearing as a line running from that feature on the map back toward you; do this for two or three features and the lines cross at your position. (3) Retracing your route — the back bearing of your outbound leg is your bearing home.`,
