    content: `Resection (loosely "triangulation") is how you find your position when you're not sure where you are but you can see identifiable landmarks. Requirements: two — better three — features that you can both SEE on the ground and locate on the map, spread out around you (ideally 60–120° apart) and unambiguous (a distinct summit, a spot height, a church spire, a road junction, a lake corner — not "that slope"). Method: (1) Take a compass bearing to feature A. (2) Convert that magnetic bearing to a grid bearing by applying the magnetic declination (or use a declination-adjusted compass). (3) Set the grid bearing on the bezel, place the compass on the map with a long edge touching feature A and the orienting lines parallel to the grid, then draw a pencil line from feature A along the compass edge — this line represents feature A's BACK bearing, and you are somewhere on it. (4) Repeat for feature B (and C). Where two lines cross is your position; three lines usually enclose a small "triangle of error" and you are within it (a large triangle means a misidentified feature or a sloppy bearing). The reverse operation, INTERSECTION, plots an unknown feature by taking forward bearings to it from two known positions. A quick partial fix: if you know you're on a linear feature (a path, a ridge, a stream), one bearing to one landmark is enough — where its line crosses the feature is you ("aspect of slope" can substitute for the second line).`,
