    content: `"Orienting" or "setting" the map means rotating the map so that its north edge faces true (or grid) north, which makes every feature drawn on the paper lie in the same compass direction as the real feature it represents — so a track that leaves to your left on the ground also leaves to the left on the map. This turns navigation from an abstract puzzle into simple matching, and it's the first thing to do at every stop. TWO METHODS: (1) WITH A COMPASS — set your compass's declination adjustment for the area (or hold it mentally). Lay the compass flat on the map with one long edge along a north–south grid line, "N" on the bezel toward the top of the map. Now turn the MAP and compass together as one unit until the red needle settles inside the orienting arrow. The map is set to north. (2) BY INSPECTION (no compass) — turn the map until a distinctive linear feature printed on it (a straight road, a field wall, a river, a ridgeline, a power line, the coast) points the same way as that same feature you can see on the ground. Faster and often enough. Keep the map set as you move and turn — don't rotate the map to keep the text upright. (This is a practical field task; it has nothing to do with map projections or Gauss's Theorema Egregium.)`,
