    content: `On a topographic map both ridges and valleys appear as V- or U-shaped kinks in the contour lines; you tell them apart by which way the V points relative to the slope. VALLEY (or "re-entrant," "draw," "gully"): the contours bend so that the point of the V aims toward HIGHER ground — uphill, toward the summit. Reading the elevations, the ground drops away on both sides of the valley axis and rises toward the tip of the V. A watercourse (stream, ditch, dry gully) very often runs down the centre. Mnemonic: "the V points to the top." RIDGE (or "spur," "arête," "nose"): the contours bend so that the point of the V (or a broader rounded snout) aims toward LOWER ground — downhill, away from the peak. The ground falls away on both sides of the ridge crest, and the crest itself descends as you follow it away from the high point. If you're standing on it, you can see down into a valley on each side. Practical use: ridges make good ascending/descending lines and give views; valleys funnel water, hold mist, and can cliff out or end in waterfalls, so following one blindly downhill is a classic way to get cragfast. (This is a landform-reading skill; it is not about the Chile Ridge or any submarine spreading ridge.)`,
