    content: `A col (from French; also called a saddle, pass, gap, notch, "bealach" in Scotland, "bwlch" in Wales) is the low point on a ridge between two adjacent summits — the lowest place you have to climb to in order to cross from one side of the ridge or mountain range to the other, and therefore where paths, roads, and railways naturally cross high ground. (It is a landform; it is not a bicycle or draisine saddle — that was a homonym error.) On a contour map a col is unmistakable: it appears as an hourglass, bow-tie, or figure-of-eight pinch, the spot where the contour rings around two neighbouring hills bulge toward each other and nearly meet. From a col, the ground rises on two opposite sides (toward the two summits) and falls away on the other two sides (into the valleys on either flank of the ridge) — a shape like a horse's saddle. In navigation a col is a valuable feature: it's a large, obvious point to aim for, it usually has a path over it, and the surrounding terrain shape confirms you've arrived. Practically, cols tend to be windy and exposed because moving air is funnelled and accelerated through the gap.`,
