    content: `A map scale is a "representative fraction": it states that 1 unit of length on the map represents a stated number of the SAME units on the ground. At 1:25,000, one centimetre on the map equals 25,000 cm on the ground = 250 m, so 4 cm = 1 km. At 1:50,000, 2 cm = 1 km. At 1:63,360 (the old "one inch to the mile"), one inch = one mile. The scale is independent of the measuring unit — 1:25,000 also means one inch on the map = 25,000 inches on the ground. LARGE vs SMALL SCALE: a "large-scale" map (1:10,000, 1:25,000) shows a small area in great detail — individual field boundaries, boulders, footbridges; a "small-scale" map (1:250,000, 1:1,000,000) shows a huge area with little detail. The larger the number after the colon, the smaller the scale and the less detail. Every map has a SCALE BAR (a graduated ruler) so you can measure real distances directly by marking the map distance on the edge of a piece of paper or a compass baseplate; the baseplate of an orienteering compass and a separate "romer" scale are printed with the common scales for reading grid references and distances. (A map scale is a ratio; it has nothing to do with fixed-point number representation in computing.)`,
