    content: `Handrailing is a core efficiency technique: instead of walking a precise compass bearing across open terrain (slow, and every degree of error compounds with distance), you deliberately pick a long, unmistakable LINEAR feature that runs roughly the way you want to go and follow alongside it — using it like a handrail on a staircase. (It is a navigation term; it has nothing to do with the metal rail bolted to a wall.) Things that make good handrails: a field wall or fence, a hedge line, the edge of a forest or plantation, a stream or river, a path, track, or road, a distinct ridge crest, a lake or reservoir shore, a power line, a wall of a valley, even a distinct line of crags. You keep the feature in view beside you (or underfoot, for a path) and simply walk, checking off features you pass along it. Handrailing lets you move fast and confidently and keeps you from getting lost, at the cost of not always taking the most direct line. You stay on the handrail until you reach an identifiable point where you must leave it (an "attack point"), then switch to careful navigation — bearing and pacing — for the short final leg to your objective.`,
