    content: `An attack point is a prominent, easy-to-locate feature situated close to a small or indistinct objective. The idea is to split a navigation leg into two phases: (1) get to the attack point FAST and LOOSELY — follow handrails, aim off, don't sweat a few degrees of bearing, because the attack point is big and unmissable; then (2) from the attack point, navigate the short final stretch SLOWLY and PRECISELY — set an exact bearing, count your paces, watch for the objective — because now there is very little distance in which to accumulate error. Examples: to find a small spring, a survey pillar, a shooting butt, a boulder, or an orienteering control marker, you might use a nearby path junction, a corner in a wall, a distinct stream bend, a lone tree, a knoll, or a fence–stream crossing as the attack point. The better the attack point (the closer to the objective and the more certain to identify), the shorter and more reliable the final leg. Choosing good attack points in advance is one of the main things separating fast, confident navigators from people who wander around a hillside searching. (An attack point is a navigation concept — it is not an aircraft's "angle of attack.")`,
