    content: `A catching feature (also "backstop," "collecting feature," "long stop") is a large, unmistakable feature that lies just BEYOND your objective, across your line of travel, and whose job is to "catch" you if you overshoot — to tell you you've gone too far so you can stop, turn around, and search back rather than walking on indefinitely into unfamiliar ground. (It has nothing to do with a Discord "catch me up" summary or with recovering from sleep debt.) Examples: if your objective is a small stream confluence in a wood, a catching feature might be the forest road, boundary wall, ridge crest, lake shore, power line, or steep drop 150–300 m past it. If your objective is a col, the catching feature is the summit or valley beyond it. Before starting a leg, a good navigator notes both an attack point (near side) and a catching feature (far side), plus any handrails and whether to aim off. Knowing there is a definite backstop removes the anxiety of navigating in poor visibility and lets you move faster and commit to the bearing, because the worst case is a short walk back.`,
