    content: `Dead reckoning (DR) is working out where you are now purely from where you started, the course you have steered, how far or how fast you have gone, and how long it has taken — with no help from landmarks, satellites, radio beacons, or celestial observations. It is the oldest form of navigation and the universal fallback for when everything else fails. ON FOOT: your DR position is your last known point plus (compass bearing walked) plus (distance from pace count) plus (a check against elapsed time and expected speed, e.g. Naismith's rule). ON A BOAT OR AIRCRAFT: start position plus heading (from the compass) plus speed (log/airspeed) plus time — then you must apply corrections for the things that push the craft sideways off the heading you're pointing: the water current ("set" and "drift") for a boat, the wind for an aircraft, which turn a heading into an actual "course made good." The defining weakness of DR is that errors — a slightly wrong bearing, an uncounted section, an unnoticed current — do not cancel out; they ACCUMULATE, so a DR position becomes steadily less trustworthy the longer you go without confirming it. Whenever you get a definite fix (identify a landmark, get a GPS reading, take a bearing resection), you "update" the DR to that point and the accumulated error resets to zero.`,
