    content: `GPS (and the other systems — GLONASS, Galileo, BeiDou, together "GNSS") works by trilateration from satellites with known positions and precise clocks. Each satellite continuously transmits a radio signal encoding two things: its own exact orbital position (from the "ephemeris" data) and the exact time of transmission, kept by onboard atomic clocks. Your receiver generates an identical copy of the satellite's code and slides it in time until it lines up with the received signal; the shift tells it how long the signal took to travel, and multiplying by the speed of light gives the distance to that satellite (a "pseudorange"). One distance puts you somewhere on a sphere centred on that satellite; two spheres intersect in a circle; three in (usually) two points, one of which is absurd. So three satellites can fix a 3-D position IF the receiver's clock were perfect — but consumer receivers have cheap quartz clocks, so a FOURTH satellite is used, and the receiver solves four equations for four unknowns: latitude, longitude, altitude, and its own clock error. More satellites (modern phones track 20–30 across constellations) improve accuracy and reliability. Corrections applied: the ionosphere and troposphere slow the signal (modelled, or measured with dual-frequency receivers); satellite orbit and clock drift (broadcast corrections, or precise post-processing); multipath from buildings; and relativity — the satellites' clocks run about 38 microseconds/day fast (7 µs slow from special relativity's velocity effect, 45 µs fast from general relativity's weaker gravity at altitude), which is pre-compensated or the fix would drift ~10 km/day.`,
