    content: `Latitude and longitude are the two angles that together give a unique position on the Earth's surface. LATITUDE is how far north or south you are of the equator, measured as an angle at the centre of the Earth: 0° at the equator, 90° N at the North Pole, 90° S at the South Pole (or −90°). Lines of equal latitude are called "parallels," they run east–west, they never meet, and they are essentially evenly spaced — one degree of latitude is about 111 km (60 nautical miles) anywhere on Earth, and one minute of latitude is one nautical mile by definition. LONGITUDE is how far east or west you are of the Prime Meridian, which by international agreement passes through Greenwich, London: 0° at Greenwich, up to 180° E and 180° W (which meet near the International Date Line). Lines of equal longitude are "meridians," they run north–south from pole to pole, and crucially they CONVERGE — so one degree of longitude is ~111 km at the equator but shrinks toward zero at the poles (multiply by the cosine of the latitude). A position is written latitude first, either as degrees-minutes-seconds (51°28′40″N, 0°00′05″W) or decimal degrees (51.4779, −0.0015). (Geographic longitude is not the same as "right ascension" in astronomy, which is measured in hours 0–24 eastward from the vernal equinox — that was a confusion in a bad answer.)`,
