    content: `UTM (Universal Transverse Mercator) is a way of giving position in flat metres instead of angles. It divides the world (from 80°S to 84°N) into 60 north–south ZONES, each 6° of longitude wide, numbered 1–60 starting at 180°W. Each zone gets its own Transverse Mercator projection and its own square metric grid, so within a zone you specify: the zone number, an EASTING (metres east of a "false origin" 500,000 m west of the zone's central meridian, so numbers stay positive), and a NORTHING (metres north of the equator; in the southern hemisphere, metres north of a false origin 10,000,000 m south, again to keep it positive). Example: "32U 545000 5250000." MGRS (Military Grid Reference System) is a more compact, human-readable encoding of the same information: the UTM zone number + a latitude-band letter (C–X, omitting I and O) + a two-letter designator for the 100 km square + an even string of digits split evenly into easting and northing. Example: "18TWL8040 1950" (zone 18, band T, 100 km square WL, then 8040 easting / 1950 northing = a 10 m box). The US National Grid (USNG) is essentially civilian MGRS. Because UTM/MGRS are metric and locally rectangular, measuring distances, bearings, and areas is simple arithmetic, and they dodge the converging-meridian problem of lat/long over any area smaller than a zone. (These are practical coordinate grids — not the abstract "discrete global grid" spatial-partitioning research a bad lookup returned.)`,
