    content: `Naismith's Rule is the classic rule of thumb for estimating how long a hill walk will take. The basic rule: allow 1 hour for every 5 km (about 3 miles) of horizontal distance, PLUS an extra 1 hour for every 600 m (2,000 ft) of ascent. An equivalent everyday phrasing: "3 miles per hour, plus half an hour per 1,000 ft of climb," or "add 1 minute for every 10 m of ascent." It was published by William W. Naismith, a Scottish mountaineer and a founder of the Scottish Mountaineering Club, in 1892. It assumes a moderately fit party, reasonable ground, daylight, and no significant stops, so it is a MINIMUM — you add time for: a heavy pack, rough/boggy/pathless ground, deep snow, scrambling, poor weather, poor visibility, a large or slow group, frequent navigation stops, photography, and rest/food breaks (many people add 25–50% overall, or a fixed break allowance). Refinements: TRANTER'S CORRECTIONS adjust the Naismith time up or down using an individual fitness rating and account for fatigue on long days; the AITKEN and LANGMUIR corrections handle descent — subtract about 10 minutes per 300 m of descent on gentle slopes (5–12°), but ADD about 10 minutes per 300 m on steep descent where you have to pick your way down. For rough planning, Naismith alone is usually within 20–30%.`,
